(ns kotoba.kir.value
  {:kotoba/export [adt-depth-limit adt-node-limit boundary-traversal-byte-limit boundary-traversal-node-limit bounded-keyword! bounded-string! bytes-byte-count bytes-from-items bytes-items bytes-slice-of bytes-value-byte-limit bytes-value? canonical-indirect-byte-limit canonical-list-item-limit canonical-list-total-item-limit canonical-tuple-item-limit compact-graph-item-limit document-container-item-limit document-depth-limit document-node-limit document-utf8-byte-limit empty-bytes f32-to-i64-bits f32-value? f64-to-i64-bits f64-value? fold-ascii! fold-case! heterogeneous-vector-item-limit i64-bits-to-f32 i64-bits-to-f64 keyword-value-byte-limit map-entry-limit record-field-limit string-index-key-byte-limit string-literal-byte-limit string-value-byte-limit symbol-value-byte-limit typed-map-entry-limit typed-set-item-limit upper-case! utf8-append-range! utf8-byte-count! utf8-code-point-at! utf8-compare! utf8-compare-lines! utf8-decode-canonical utf8-find-blank! utf8-find-byte! utf8-index-of! utf8-index-of-from! utf8-skip-blank! utf8-substring! variant-case-limit vector-item-limit vector-literal-item-limit]}
  (:require [kotoba.lang.text :as str]
            )
  )

;; UTF-8 bytes of one string value: 8 MiB (8 388 608) since 2026-10-02, amu
;; ADR 0362 (was 64 KiB). A self-built compiler reads each of its sources as
;; ONE string, and the source admission bound is 8 MiB (amu ADR 0356); a
;; compiler that could admit a source it could not hold is refusing by
;; accident. kotoba-lang lang/limits.edn :language/value :string-value-bytes.
(defn string-value-byte-limit [] :i64 8388608)
;; A literal is a value, but the bound a string may be WRITTEN as is a code-size
;; bound of its own: 64 KiB (it was 4096, 16x below the then value bound, and
;; refused 3 of the compiler's own sources, a .wit file held in a def, 5042
;; bytes; measured 2026-09-11, adr-2609111900). It stayed 65536 when the value
;; bound moved to 8 MiB (amu ADR 0362): literals live in the module's rodata,
;; which ADR 0359 bounds as a whole. lang/limits.edn :language/static
;; :string-literal-bytes.
(defn string-literal-byte-limit [] :i64 65536)
;; Opaque binary payload bound for kit/runtime `:bytes` (stream-object
;; max-pull-bytes). Same magnitude as string-value-byte-limit deliberately: a
;; source read as :bytes and decoded is the same 8 MiB (amu ADR 0362).
;; lang/limits.edn :language/value :bytes-value-bytes.
(defn bytes-value-byte-limit [] :i64 8388608)
(defn keyword-value-byte-limit [] :i64 512)
(defn symbol-value-byte-limit [] :i64 512)
(defn map-entry-limit [] :i64 128)
(defn vector-literal-item-limit [] :i64 128)
;; How many items one `vector-i64` / `vector-f64` may hold.
;;
;; 16,384 for as long as every element write COPIED the whole vector: the
;; native host memmoved and bump-allocated, the Wasm host did `[...checked]`
;; and froze, and `assertValue` re-scanned every element on every operation.
;; Under that, a bigger vector only bought a bigger copy -- raising this would
;; have made programs slower rather than possible.
;;
;; `vector-assoc!` removes the copy and the linear-value scan skip removes the
;; per-operation walk (superproject ADR-2609010200), so the number is now set
;; by what a guest needs rather than by what a copy costs.
;;
;; 1,048,576 because that is `torihiki.book`'s default `cap` -- one slot per
;; resting order in a struct of arrays. Chosen against a real program rather
;; than rounded up: a limit nobody's workload explains is a limit nobody can
;; argue with when it turns out to be wrong.
;;
;; Still a limit and still fail-closed. 8 MB per i64 field is a real cost, and
;; a guest asking for more is refused rather than allowed to exhaust the host.
;;
;; 16,777,216 (2^24) since 2026-09-16, owner decision (`vector-item-limit も
;; security 的に問題なければ向上`), with the argument the 2026-09-01 note
;; below asked for. The number is the largest ONE vector may be on any host,
;; and each host bounds it a second time by what it was given:
;;
;;   * the native loader's item arena is a per-run BUDGET (KEXE_VECTOR_ITEMS,
;;     `package-command --vector-items`; 65,536 words unless the packager
;;     asks, refused above 2^27 words = 1 GiB), so a vector can never be
;;     longer than the run was packaged to hold, and `vector-alloc` past the
;;     budget traps `:vector-items-exhausted` before a byte is written;
;;   * this interpreter allocates per element, so the constant bounds one
;;     value at 128 MB (2^24 x 8), the largest single allocation a reference
;;     execution can be asked for; the analysis-time constant oracle runs
;;     under 100,000 fuel and cannot fill it;
;;   * the Wasm host copies on write without `vector-assoc!`, which makes a
;;     large vector SLOW there, not unsafe -- fuel and the linear memory
;;     bound it as before.
;;
;; What the number buys, measured: the line-start table of a 33 MB / 769,400-
;; line sort (org-ieee-sort's index merge sort, 2026-09-16), which 16,384
;; refused; 2^20 (the order book's `cap`) would admit that file and refuse a
;; 1.5 GB one that the 1 GiB pool cannot hold anyway, so the constant is set
;; where the ARENA's maximum is rather than at one workload.
(defn vector-item-limit [] :i64 16777216)
;; Raised to 1048576 for the order book above, then put back on 2026-09-01.
;; The in-place lowering had to land first -- raising the ceiling before it
;; only makes each copy bigger -- and it now has, so the argument above is no
;; longer premature. It was still a SEPARATE decision: lang/surface-status.edn
;; recorded :arena-bounds with its own fail-closed argument owing, and amu's
;; component test asserted that a length one past this traps in the core.
;; Raising it silently inside the lowering change made that test pass for
;; the wrong reason -- the guest was admitted, not refused -- which is the
;; shape this file exists to prevent. Raised 2026-09-16 with the native
;; arena, the host budget and that test in one wave, arguing for the number
;; above.
;; Canonical ABI `[:list item-descriptor]` item-count bound (see
;; kotoba.wasm.canonical-abi's `list-layout`). Deliberately the same
;; order of magnitude as this file's other bounded-sequential-collection
;; limits (`vector-item-limit` above) rather than a freshly invented number;
;; kept as its own named constant rather than a direct reuse of
;; `vector-item-limit` because the two bound genuinely different domains
;; (a `vector-i64` runtime value vs. a Canonical ABI wire-transport schema
;; shape whose item type is not restricted to i64) that happen to share a
;; magnitude today, not one concept wearing two names.
(defn canonical-list-item-limit [] :i64 16384)
;; Shared across every nested `[:list ...]` node in one value graph. Keeping
;; this equal to the flat per-list ceiling preserves existing flat-list
;; capacity while preventing list-of-list cardinalities from multiplying.
(defn canonical-list-total-item-limit [] :i64 16384)
;; A collection of individually valid strings must not multiply the per-leaf
;; bound into an unbounded host allocation. All string/keyword leaves in one
;; canonical typed value share this aggregate UTF-8 payload budget.
(defn canonical-indirect-byte-limit [] :i64 1048576)
;; Raised 8 → 12 for T8.3/W4 structured key/value pair spines in recursive
;; EDN ADTs (pair-of-entry-pairs for header maps + request/result spines).
;; Document depth stays at document-depth-limit 8. ADR kotoba-kir 0025.
;;
;; Since P2 of superproject adr-2609242100 (2026-09-24) these two bound a TYPE
;; DESCRIPTOR (`validate-value-type!`, checked statically), not a value: a
;; value has no depth or node limit, and an untrusted one is walked once
;; against `boundary-traversal-node-limit` / `-byte-limit` instead. What bounds
;; a value built inside a run is the budget its caller granted: every
;; constructor debits the run's fuel (amu ADR 0353 point 7, kotoba.kir
;; `cell-constructor-ops`), so a tree is as large as it was paid for.
(defn adt-depth-limit [] :i64 64)
(defn adt-node-limit [] :i64 512)
(defn variant-case-limit [] :i64 32)
(defn heterogeneous-vector-item-limit [] :i64 32)
(defn typed-set-item-limit [] :i64 32)
(defn typed-map-entry-limit [] :i64 31)
(defn record-field-limit [] :i64 32)
;; Canonical ABI `[:tuple item-descriptor ...]` item-count bound (see
;; kotoba.wasm.canonical-abi's `tuple-layout`). A tuple is a structural,
;; positional analog of a sealed record's fields, so this is kept at the same
;; magnitude as this file's own nominal `record-field-limit` above (a sealed
;; record's own field-count bound) and `heterogeneous-vector-item-limit`
;; above (this file's pre-existing fixed-length heterogeneous-product bound,
;; which `kotoba.component.wit` already renders to this exact WIT
;; `tuple<...>` syntax today) -- both already 32. Kept as its own named
;; constant rather than a direct reuse of either, for the same reason
;; `canonical-list-item-limit` above got its own name alongside
;; `vector-item-limit`: these bound genuinely different domains that happen
;; to share a magnitude today, not one concept wearing three names.
(defn canonical-tuple-item-limit [] :i64 32)
(defn compact-graph-item-limit [] :i64 128)
(defn string-index-key-byte-limit [] :i64 65536)
(defn document-depth-limit [] :i64 8)
;; Raised 256 -> 4096 on 2026-09-10, and given the reason it never had.
;;
;; The BOUND is a property: a document must be structurally bounded, and
;; `document-utf8-byte-limit` below does not provide it -- that budget charges
;; text only, so a document of 65,536 empty nodes passes it. This is the only
;; thing bounding the shape.
;;
;; The NUMBER was not derived. 256 sat beside `canonical-list-total-item-limit`
;; 16384 and `canonical-indirect-byte-limit` 1048576 in this same file, two and
;; four orders of magnitude looser, with nothing saying why documents were the
;; tight one.
;;
;; What it cost: a Kotoba screen. Measured in adr-2608690000, the todo layout
;; filled at about SEVEN ROWS, and that ADR says outright the answer is "too
;; small to claim cljs equivalence". Measured again independently on
;; 2026-09-10 with a four-column table: 7 rows at 256, 123 at 4096, 399 at
;; 16384.
;;
;; Why 4096 and not more: it is the smallest power of two that clears a
;; realistic screen with headroom, it stays 4x below this file's own aggregate
;; structural bound (16384), and it is cheap -- validating 33,825 nodes takes
;; 8.5 ms and 11.7 MB, so 4096 is roughly 1.5 ms and 1.5 MB. Raising it further
;; is now a one-line change with a guard behind it, which is the argument for
;; being conservative here rather than generous.
;;
;; ⚠ This number is RESTATED in `kotoba.script` as `max-document-nodes`,
;; because the ESM emitter writes it into every artifact it emits and an
;; emitted module has no classpath. Changing one alone compiles and then traps
;; at runtime -- measured, which is why amu's `value_bounds_agreement_test`
;; now compares them.
(defn document-node-limit [] :i64 4096)
(defn document-container-item-limit [] :i64 32)
(defn document-utf8-byte-limit [] :i64 65536)

(defn f64-value? [value :f64] :bool true)

(defn f32-value? [value :f32] :bool true)

(defn f32-to-i64-bits [value :f32] :i64 (f32-to-bits value))

(defn i64-bits-to-f32 [bits :i64] :f32 (f32-from-bits bits))

nil

nil

nil

nil

(defn f64-to-i64-bits [value :f64] :i64 (f64-to-bits value))

(defn i64-bits-to-f64 [bits :i64] :f64 (f64-from-bits bits))

